.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="Gibbs&#x27;s phase rule, F = C - P + 2 (~chemistrykit.thermo.systems.phase_equilibria.gibbs_phase_rule), says how many intensive variables can change while a set of phases coexists. On a one-component phase diagram, single-phase regions are areas (F=2), two-phase boundaries are curves (F=1), and three phases meet only at a point (F=0). The sketch below draws water&#x27;s diagram around its triple point (vaporization and sublimation curves from the Clausius-Clapeyron equation, melting line from the Clapeyron slope) and labels each feature with its degrees of freedom.">

.. only:: html

  .. image:: /api/gallery/thermo/phase_equilibria/images/thumb/sphx_glr_plot_02_gibbs_phase_rule_thumb.png
